Article 22

Assistance services as referred to in Article 20 paragraph (1) letter b, shall include:
a. accompanying victims during the examination and health recovery process;
b. accompanying victims during the medico-legal process;
c. accompanying victims during the mediation process, examination at the Police, Prosecutor's Office and Court;
d. monitoring the interests and rights of victims in the examination process at the Police, Prosecutor's Office and Court;
e. maintaining the privacy and confidentiality of victims from all parties who have no interest, including coverage by the mass media;
f. coordinating with other assistants; and
g. providing continuous handling up to the rehabilitation stage.

Article 20

1. Services to victims as referred to in Article 19, include:
a. complaint, consultation, and counseling services;
b. assistance services;
c. health services;
d. social rehabilitation services;
e. legal aid services;
f. psychological services; and
g. repatriation and social reintegration services.
2. Forms of services as referred to in paragraph ( 1) are implemented by UPTD-PPA and may cooperate with related Regional Apparatus and the community.

Article 23

1. Health services as referred to in Article 20 letter c constitute efforts that include promotive, preventive, curative, and rehabilitative aspects both for basic health services and referrals.
2. Health services as referred to in paragraph (1) shall include:
a. first aid to victims;
b. examination and treatment;
c. follow-up examination of victims;
d. coordination of health rehabilitation implementation;
e. medico-legal examination including collection of evidence from victims and preparation of visum et repertum;
f. supporting and laboratory examination of evidence;
g. specialist doctor consultation or making referrals; and
h. case reporting.

Article 17

1. Prevention of violence against women and children is carried out in an integrated manner by the Regional Government coordinated by the regional apparatus that has the main duties and functions of coordination in the field of women's empowerment and child protection.
2. Prevention efforts as referred to in paragraph ( 1) are implemented by:
a. forming a network in violence prevention efforts;
b. conducting coordination, integration, synchronization of violence prevention based on partnership patterns;
c. providing counseling/guidance;
d. conducting socialization about laws and regulations related to the protection of women and children victims of violence; and
e. providing critical education about the rights of women and children to the community.

Article 34

1. In order to ensure the effectiveness of the implementation of KLA development policies in the Region, a KLA Task Force shall be established.
(2) The KLA Task Force as referred to in paragraph (1) shall have the main tasks, namely:
a. coordinating the implementation of KLA policies and development;
b. determining the tasks of KLA Task Force members;
c. conducting socialization, advocacy, and communication of information and education on KLA policies;
d. collecting baseline data;
e. conducting needs analysis sourced from baseline data;
f. disseminating baseline data;
g. determining the focus and priorities of programs in realizing KLA, which are adjusted to the potential of the Region;
h. preparing a regional action plan and KLA working mechanism for 5 (five) years; and
i. conducting monitoring, evaluation, and reporting at least once (1) a year.
3. Further provisions regarding the procedures for appointment and dismissal of KLA Task Force membership shall be regulated by Regent Regulation.

Article 33

1. Fulfillment of children's rights shall be implemented in an integrated and systematic manner from all sectors on an ongoing basis through KLA development policies.

(2) KLA development policies as referred to in paragraph (1) shall contain provisions regarding:
a. the KLA concept;
b. children's rights; and
c. the KLA development approach.
3. KLA development policies as referred to in paragraph (1) shall be directed at the fulfillment of children's rights, including:
a. civil rights and freedoms;
b. family environment and alternative care;
c. basic health and welfare;
d. education, leisure time utilization, and cultural activities; and
e. special protection.
4. Stages of KLA development as referred to in paragraph (1) shall include:
a. preparation;
b. planning;
c. implementation;
d. monitoring;
e. evaluation; and
f. reporting.

(5) Further provisions regarding KLA development policies shall be regulated by Regent Regulation.

CHAPTER IV

RIGHTS OF VICTIMS

Article 12

Every victim has the right to:
a. have their dignity respected as a human being;
b. obtain health and psychological recovery;
c. determine their own decisions;
d. obtain information;
e. obtain special handling related to victim confidentiality;
f. obtain restitution;
g. obtain social rehabilitation;
h. obtain complaint handling services;

i. obtain assistance at every level of the process in accordance with the provisions of the legislation; and/or
j. obtain facilitation in the judicial process.

Article 18

( 1) Prevention of violence against women and children as referred to in Article I 7 is implemented by Regional Apparatus and UPTD whose main duties and functions are in the field of:
a. education;

